## Worked routing examples

### Example 1 — "Show user streak in profile screen and persist it"

1. Cross-domain (data + UI) → use `@<PROJECT_SLUG>-orchestrator`
2. Read `swiftui-patterns.md` + `coredata-schema.md`
3. Orchestrator delegates: `ios-data` first (schema migration) → `ios-ui` second (binding)
4. Each specialist auto-loads their instruction file when editing matching paths

### Example 2 — "VoiceOver order is wrong on ProfileView"

1. UI-only → use `@<PROJECT_SLUG>-ios-ui` directly (no orchestrator needed)
2. Read `swiftui-patterns.md` § Accessibility
3. `swiftui.instructions.md` auto-loads when editing `Sources/Views/ProfileView.swift`

### Example 3 — "Add IDFA-using analytics SDK"

1. Privacy-sensitive → orchestrator routes through `<PROJECT_SLUG>-ios-privacy` (REVIEW-ONLY) FIRST for risk audit
2. Then `<PROJECT_SLUG>-ios-network` for SDK init
3. Then `<PROJECT_SLUG>-ios-release` for ATT prompt + nutrition label update

### Example 4 — "TestFlight build failed code signing"

1. Release-only → `@<PROJECT_SLUG>-ios-release`
2. Read `release-process.md`, `code-signing.instructions.md` auto-loads
3. Likely cause: provisioning profile expired or Match cache invalid

### Example 5 — "Crashlytics top issue this week"

1. Triage → `@<PROJECT_SLUG>-ios-perf`
2. Read `performance-baseline.md`
3. Perf specialist identifies hotspot, hands off fix to relevant specialist (likely `ios-ui` or `ios-data`)

